# Aspirin

Everybody called her Aspirin -- she always looked like she was aspiring to become a special state of being, and just looking at her made your headache go away.

Her real name was Astrid Pine, which the neighborhood children had mangled into "Aspirin" sometime around third grade, and the name had stuck with the tenacity of wet leaves on a windshield. By the time she was seventeen, even her mother used it, calling up the stairs on Sunday mornings: "Aspirin, honey, pancakes are getting cold!"

She lived at the end of Marigold Lane in a house the color of weak tea, with a garden that seemed to grow whatever it pleased -- daffodils in October, a stubborn rosebush that bloomed straight through a January frost. The neighbors said the garden didn't know the rules. The neighbors said a lot of things about Aspirin.

What they meant, mostly, was that she was strange in the way that certain people are strange -- not off-putting, but slightly out of focus, like a photograph taken a half-second before everyone else was ready. She wore her grandmother's old cardigans in summer. She named the crows that visited her fence and claimed they gossiped about the mailman. She kept a notebook of other people's headaches.

That was the thing nobody knew about. The notebook.

It started when she was twelve, at the pharmacy with her father, watching an old woman clutch her temple while waiting for a prescription. Aspirin had felt the woman's pain the way you feel rain coming -- a pressure behind the eyes, a heaviness in the air. She'd gone home and written it down: *Tuesday. Gray coat. Pain like a fist slowly closing. Loneliness, mostly.*

She got good at it. She could walk through the grocery store and read the headaches like headlines. The man in aisle four: *tension, work-related, the specific ache of a man who has said yes when he meant no for eleven years.* The teenage girl by the oranges: *the kind that starts at the base of the skull, the weight of being perceived.*

She never told anyone what the headaches were. She just knew. And somehow -- this was the part she couldn't explain, the part she never wrote down -- when people talked to her, even briefly, even about nothing, the headaches lifted. The old woman at the pharmacy stopped her in the parking lot a week later and said, "I don't know what it is about you, sweetheart, but I felt better for days."

So she became the neighborhood's unofficial remedy. Mr. Okafor from two doors down would come by when his migraines got bad, and they'd sit on the porch and talk about nothing -- the weather, the crows, whether tomatoes were technically a fruit -- and he'd leave looking ten years younger. Mrs. Delgado brought her lemon bars every time her son disappointed her, and they'd sit in comfortable silence while the weight behind her eyes dissolved.

"You're going to waste yourself on us," Mrs. Delgado said once, watching Aspirin sketch in her notebook. "A girl like you. You should be somewhere important."

But Aspirin had done the math. She'd filled seven notebooks by her eighteenth birthday, and she'd noticed something: the headaches were getting worse everywhere. Not just on Marigold Lane. In the newspapers. In the faces of strangers on the bus. The whole world seemed to be pressing its temples.

She figured somebody had to keep notes.

The autumn she turned nineteen, a developer bought the lot behind her house and put up one of those buildings that looks like every other building being built that year -- gray, angular, aggressively ordinary. The construction gave the whole street headaches for three months. Aspirin sat on her roof with her notebook and catalogued them all: *the foreman, who missed his daughter; the young guy with the tattoo, who was scared he'd made a terrible mistake; the woman engineer, whose headache was actually relief that she'd finally left, disguised as stress.*

When the building finished, a man moved into the top floor. He was young, maybe thirty, and he had the worst headache Aspirin had ever seen. She could feel it from her garden, radiating down like heat off a stove. It wasn't a sharp pain. It was the deep, structural kind -- the headache of someone who had built a life that didn't fit, and knew it, and kept building anyway.

She wrote about him for two weeks before she ever spoke to him.

He noticed her first, actually. She was in the garden at dusk, talking to the crows, and he was on his balcony with a glass of wine he wasn't drinking, and he called down: "Do they answer?"

"Constantly," she said. "But never about anything important. Crows are terrible gossips. All drama, no substance."

He laughed -- a surprised sound, like he'd forgotten how -- and she felt his headache shift, just slightly, like a knot loosening one thread.

His name was Marcus. He worked in finance, which he described as "being paid to move numbers around until they mean nothing." He'd moved to the city for a job that looked perfect on paper and felt like a shoe on the wrong foot. He came down to the garden the next evening, and the next, and eventually he was there most evenings, sitting on the porch steps while Aspirin weeded and talked and the crows offered their unsolicited opinions.

"You know what's strange," he said one night in October. "I've had a headache for three years. Doctors, scans, the whole thing. And it's just -- gone. Since I started coming here."

Aspirin pulled a weed and considered the crows, who were watching her with what she swore was knowing amusement.

"Maybe you just needed someone to talk to," she said.

"Maybe," he said. But he looked at her like she was a puzzle he wasn't trying to solve, which was, she'd later think, exactly the right way to look at her.

Here is what Aspirin never told anyone, what she kept in the very back of her seventh notebook, in handwriting so small it looked like an apology:

*I don't think I take the headaches away. I think I just remind people they were allowed to put them down.*

She thought about this a lot, that winter. About how everyone carried their pains like identity documents, presenting them at every border: *this is who I am, this is what's wrong with me, this is the weight I bear.* And maybe the trick wasn't medicine at all. Maybe the trick was being the kind of person who looked at you and saw something other than the headache -- someone who talked to you about crows and tomatoes and the weather, who acted like your pain was not the most interesting thing about you, who simply expected, in some quiet way, for you to be well.

Maybe healing was just being seen without your headache and realizing you'd been carrying it in your hands, and your hands had been free the whole time.

In the spring, Marcus quit his job. He started a small company that helped people leaving finance figure out what they actually wanted, which was, he said, "basically what you do, but with worse jokes and a business license." Mrs. Delgado's son came home. Mr. Okafor's migraines thinned out to almost nothing. The crows had babies, who were, Aspirin reported, even worse gossips than their parents.

And Aspirin kept her notebooks. She was up to eleven now, shelved in her grandmother's old cardigan drawer, a quiet archive of a neighborhood's pain, each entry ending the same way, in her small, careful hand:

*Better now.*

The headaches never stopped coming, of course. The world kept pressing its temples. But on Marigold Lane, at least, there was a house the color of weak tea, and a garden that didn't know the rules, and a woman everyone called Aspirin, who had figured out the only secret worth keeping:

We are all aspiring to become a special state of being. Most of us just need someone to tell us we've already started. [end of text]


